#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5dab76a-9d0c-5625-bba5-f903a55e3b7d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Infinite Behavior of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "8ad1be49-273c-572d-8221-9605bfa16b7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4012" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following is true when $x\\rightarrow 2$?\n\n1. $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$\n2. $f(x) \\rightarrow 2$\n3. $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$\n\n![](images/33/33650d66a0f3a2fae762b1c19da952597740868b00eeec11a14ad822536962e8.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $2$, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\n![](images/45/4585f983dac68f3ac655fbaaed6481cd1479c4cffae2c10d9f08d991c6533f51.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129103 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "408f96cb-5b2d-532b-874b-f9441dddfa30" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing One-Sided Infinite Behavior of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9d4d4b06-ace1-5d4a-828a-decea7c5b5fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4053" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d5750d5909f8a6e7c893109dd2ebe1e6317e7e5505df52d8be8966b8f914f140.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is true when $x \\rightarrow 1^{+}$?\n\n1. $f(x) \\rightarrow -2$\n2. $f(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$\n3. $f(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $x\\rightarrow 1^{+}$ means that $x$ approaches $1$ *from the right*.\n\n![](images/99/99421620750831182ef45a4cfda34ee4b36f772f88df5572783bffc3ea9580c5.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow 1^{+}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129104 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b81a25e-32d1-5f5f-9f8e-b18e5d354e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/id #uuid "5dee2a1e-7dd9-5c99-9f3b-5189360a2ef5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4054" :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y = f(x)$ shown below. What is the equation of its vertical asymptote?\n\n![](images/63/63fe6fd9a6a830b4f3f446d265ae57c24bec2e5f23917c4a5dda4000c63198e8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $x = a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y = f(x)$ if $f(x) \\to \\pm \\infty$ as $x \\to a^+$ or $x \\to a^-$.\n\n![](images/59/596435060b73c6df3b05bf5f56b4650c2e77ecb70203f3d680ad9d0dddc1f027.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$. Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty$.\n\nSo, we can write:\n\n- $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -1^-$\n- $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -1^+$\n\nThese give us the same asymptote $x = -1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29ea673-5a84-5e48-b45d-85cade99566d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where a Function Approaches Infinity"}] :question/id #uuid "b6212c1b-c1c8-5d0d-9984-1509b721e54d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4062" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a28f89ca66be27ba85ded32668b7077cb2b152f9877fe52f6702a40be047773.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Find the value of $b-a$, given the following:\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow a$\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow b$" :question/worked-solution "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$, the values of $g(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty$.\n\n![](images/c5/c5b5723c08edacff5a29e160b225343f565d23bb764a84149ca20ebbe28d4ad3.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n$g(x) \\to -\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -1$.\n\nTherefore, $a = -1$.\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$, the values of $g(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf9f0d83f05002952d531fe1dcaa6ad14d7d7c4216a59d9a2d3a4e621b57ef5.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n$g(x) \\to \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to 1$.\n\nTherefore, $b =1$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb - a = 1 - (-1) = 2\n$$"}]}
